# Lab 04 – Introduction to SCADA

## Overview

This lab introduces Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) systems and demonstrates the connection between a SCADA Human-Machine Interface (HMI) and an OpenPLC Runtime using Modbus TCP.

The lab uses a simulated tank-fill process to observe how process information is presented to an operator through an HMI.

## Objectives

- Understand the purpose and architecture of SCADA systems.
- Identify the roles of PLCs, HMIs, alarms, events, and historized process data.
- Configure and run a FUXA SCADA HMI.
- Connect FUXA to OpenPLC Runtime using Modbus TCP.
- Observe the automated tank-fill process.
- Interpret operator-facing process indications and trends.
- Understand the relationship between field processes, PLC control, industrial communication, and SCADA visualization.

## SCADA Architecture

The laboratory demonstrates the following control and monitoring flow:

Field/Process → PLC → Modbus TCP Network → SCADA/HMI → Operator

The PLC performs the control logic for the process, while the SCADA/HMI layer provides visualization and operator-facing information.

## Laboratory Components

- OpenPLC Runtime
- FUXA SCADA/HMI
- Modbus TCP
- Tank-fill simulation
- PLC
- Operator HMI

## Network Configuration

| System | Address |
|---|---|
| PLC VM | 192.168.1.10 |
| SCADA VM | 192.168.2.10 |
| Modbus TCP Port | 502 |

## HMI Process

The FUXA HMI is used to display the tank-fill simulation and provide operator-facing indications such as:

- Tank level
- Valve status
- Process trends
- Alarms or other process indications

## PLC and SCADA Roles

The PLC is responsible for executing the real-time control logic of the industrial process. The SCADA/HMI system provides visualization and supervisory information to the operator.

This separation allows the control system to continue performing its programmed control functions while the SCADA layer provides monitoring and operator context.

